# Page metadata: tab titles, icons, and link previews

Status: proposed. Written while the owner was away. Calls I made on my own are marked **Assumed** and listed together at the end so they can be confirmed or reversed.

This covers bugs 233, 240, 247, and 251 in `docs/issues.md`. The repo has no app code yet, so the root causes below come from the bug reports and from how Tessel is described in `docs/brief.md`. They are not taken from code.

## How a page is served

Each page address returns **Tessel's wrapper page**: the toolbar plus an iframe that runs the author's HTML. Everything that browsers and link-preview tools read about a page (the `<title>`, the icon links, Open Graph and card tags, robots directives) comes from the **wrapper**, not from the author's HTML. Two facts follow:

- Browser tabs show the wrapper's title and icon. A `<title>` or `<link rel="icon">` inside the iframe has no effect on the tab. That is bug 240.
- Link-preview tools fetch the wrapper's first HTML response and run no script. They never see the author's document, and certainly not anything the author's JavaScript builds. That is bug 247.

**Assumed:** the author's HTML is served from a separate content origin on its own registrable domain, never from Tessel's app origin, and the wrapper embeds it in a sandboxed iframe. This matters for security apart from metadata: author code must never run on the origin that holds Tessel session cookies. The rest of this design assumes the wrapper and the page are cross-origin and can talk only through `postMessage`.

## Root causes and fixes at a glance

| Bug | Likely cause | Fix |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 233: a private page's title and first paragraph appeared in a chat preview | The wrapper rendered metadata before checking access, or rendered it for anyone who requested the page | Check access before rendering any metadata. Private pages never emit preview tags and never put their title in server HTML (see [Private](#private)) |
| 240: every tab shows the Tessel icon and "Tessel" | The wrapper's head is static | Server-render the page's own title and icon into the wrapper, and pass live changes up from the runtime (see [Tab title and icon](#tab-title-and-icon)) |
| 247: previews of public pages are blank | Preview tags missing from the wrapper's first response, and pages built by JavaScript have no static title to extract | Pull metadata out at publish time. When the static HTML has none, a sandboxed render fills it in. The wrapper always renders the tags on the server (see [Where metadata comes from](#where-metadata-comes-from)) |
| 251: a poll gained a vote each time its link was pasted | A preview tool that runs JavaScript loaded the page, and the page wrote data without any person interacting | The runtime refuses writes until a real person has interacted with the page. Loading a page never writes anything (see [Loading a page never writes](#loading-a-page-never-writes)) |

## Where metadata comes from

The brief says nobody has to write a manifest or make a preview image. So the author's HTML file is the only place an author sets metadata, using standard tags they would write anyway. Tessel infers whatever is missing.

**Assumed:** v1 has no settings form inside Tessel for title, description, or image. If there were one, there would be two authored sources for the same value. To change how a page presents itself, the author or their agent edits the HTML. A settings override can be added later. If it is, it outranks the HTML.

Each field is resolved in this order. The first value found wins, so author-written values always beat generated ones and generated values beat defaults:

| Field | 1. Declared in the author's HTML | 2. Generated (public and unlisted only) | 3. Default |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Title | `og:title`, then `<title>`, then the first `<h1>` | `document.title`, then the first `<h1>`, from a sandboxed render | The page's name from its address, or "Untitled page" |
| Description | `og:description`, then `<meta name="description">` | The first meaningful text block from the render, trimmed to about 160 characters | "An interactive page on Tessel." |
| Icon | `<link rel="icon">` (any variant), `apple-touch-icon` | None | Tessel's icon |
| Preview image | `og:image` / `twitter:image` | The Tessel card template (see [Preview images](#preview-images)) | Same as generated |

### Extracting at publish time

Each time a page version is published, a parser reads the static HTML and stores the declared values with that version. This stored copy is a cache of the HTML, so it is keyed by version and replaced on every publish rather than edited. Rules:

- Titles: strip control characters, collapse whitespace, cap at 120 characters. Descriptions: cap at 300.
- `data:` URLs for icons and images are common in single-file pages. Decode them, check the type (PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP, ICO, SVG) and size (icon at most 256 KB, image at most 5 MB), and store them in object storage under an opaque, content-addressed key. Serve them from the content or asset origin. SVGs are served with `Content-Security-Policy: sandbox` and are sanitized, so opening one directly cannot run script.
- Absolute `https:` URLs are passed through as written. Relative URLs and any other scheme are ignored, because a single HTML file has nothing for a relative URL to point to.

### Generated values (the sandboxed render)

This fixes bug 247 for pages that build their content with JavaScript. It runs as a background job, and only when all of these hold:

- the page is public or unlisted (private pages are never rendered for metadata), **and**
- the static parse found no title or no description, **and**
- the version is new or the visibility just changed from private.

The job loads the content URL in a headless browser with the runtime in **preview mode**. Reads return the page's current data. Writes resolve successfully inside the sandbox but never persist. The job waits for load plus a short settle period (about 3 s, with a hard cap of 10 s), then reads `document.title`, the first `<h1>`, and the first meaningful text. Its user agent identifies it as Tessel's preview renderer. Job states are `pending → ready | failed`. A failure gets one automatic retry and then falls back to defaults. The job carries a version token and exits without writing if a newer version was published while it ran. The wrapper never waits for the job. It renders whatever is available now.

## Tab title and icon

### Format

The tab reads `{page title} · Tessel`, with the page's own subject first and the product last, using one separator everywhere. **Assumed:** keeping the suffix is acceptable to authors. It also helps against pages that title themselves to impersonate another site. The preview title uses the same string as the tab.

### Initial value

For public and unlisted pages, the wrapper puts the resolved title and icon into its server-rendered `<head>`, so the first paint and every preview tool see them. Private pages differ (see [Private](#private)).

### Live changes

Pages change their title and icon at runtime ("(3) new votes", a status icon). The Tessel runtime inside the iframe watches `<title>` and `<link rel~="icon">` with a `MutationObserver` and posts `{ type: "tessel:head", title, icon }` to the parent. The wrapper:

- accepts the message only when `event.source` is its own iframe and `event.origin` is the content origin;
- applies the same cleaning and length cap as extraction, then sets `document.title = "{title} · Tessel"`;
- accepts an icon only as a `data:image/*` URL up to 256 KB or as an `https:` URL;
- re-applies the title whenever the wrapper's own framework rewrites the head, so a re-render cannot quietly revert it.

These live values are display only. They are never saved back as metadata, because any visitor could forge them.

## Preview images

- When the author declares `og:image`, that image is used.
- Otherwise Tessel serves a **generated card** at 1200×630. It shows the Tessel mark, the name "Tessel", and the page's resolved title, in Tessel's colors and type. The image is derived, not stored. It is rendered on request at `/{page address}/card.png?v={hash of title + visibility + template version}` and cached at the CDN. A title change changes the URL, so preview tools fetch the new image.
- **Assumed:** no screenshot of the page in v1. A screenshot would need the headless render on every publish. It would also freeze live data, such as vote counts, into an image that goes stale, and it could show content the author did not mean to put in a preview. Authors who want a picture can declare `og:image`. Screenshots can be added later as a generated value that ranks below `og:image`.
- The card route enforces visibility on every request, using the same check the wrapper uses. Private, deleted, and nonexistent pages get `404`.

## Behavior by visibility

All of this comes from one shared function, `resolvePageMetadata(page, viewer)`, in a domain package. The wrapper route and the card route both call it, so access is checked in one place. Its result says which tags to emit, not only what goes in them.

### Public

- `200`. Server-rendered `<title>`, icon links, `<meta name="description">`, `<link rel="canonical">`, Open Graph (`og:title`, `og:description`, `og:image`, `og:url`, `og:site_name=Tessel`, `og:type=website`), and `twitter:card=summary_large_image` with its title, description, and image. A base URL is set so every URL in these tags is absolute.
- Indexable and listed in the sitemap.
- The wrapper's server HTML includes a `<noscript>` block with the title and description as a heading and a paragraph, so clients without JavaScript and crawlers get real content.
- CDN-cacheable with a short TTL. The cache is purged on publish and on any visibility change.

### Unlisted

- Same title, icon, and preview as public. Anyone with the link can open the page, and preview tools ignore indexing directives anyway, so a card reveals nothing that opening the link would not.
- `noindex` both as `<meta name="robots">` and as `X-Robots-Tag`, including on the card image and on re-hosted assets. Left out of the sitemap. Canonical points to itself.

### Private

The goal is that no title, description, or image ever reaches a preview tool, even one that sends the sharer's cookies.

- **No member session:** the response is a sign-in screen, the same one served for an address that does not exist. Its `<title>` is `Tessel`, it uses the Tessel icon, and it has no description, Open Graph, or card tags. So the preview names only the product and does not confirm that a private page exists.
- **Signed in, but not a member:** the same "not available" screen as for a nonexistent page, with the same generic head. **Assumed:** no "request access" flow in v1, because one would confirm the page exists.
- **Member:** the server-rendered head is still the generic `Tessel` title and icon, with no description or preview tags. After the wrapper's script boots, it sets the real title and icon on the client. Tools that build previews do not run script, so even one that carries a member's cookies sees only "Tessel". The member sees the real title in the tab immediately. The tab is fine to show because the member is already looking at the page. Descriptions and images travel further than a tab, so private pages never get them.
- Always sent: `Cache-Control: private, no-store`, `X-Robots-Tag: noindex`, `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`. The referrer policy stops the page address from leaking to third parties the page loads. Never in the sitemap. The sandboxed render never runs for a private page, and the card route returns `404`.

### When a page becomes less visible, or is deleted

- Public → unlisted: add `noindex`, remove the page from the sitemap, purge the CDN.
- Public or unlisted → private, or deleted: the wrapper and card switch immediately to the generic fallback or `404`. Purge the CDN entries for the wrapper, the card, and re-hosted assets. Re-hosted assets for a private page are served only to members, and deleted pages' assets are removed.
- Tessel cannot recall previews that other apps already cached. The visibility control should say so in a short line ("Previews already posted elsewhere may still show the old title."). **Assumed** copy, to be confirmed.

## Loading a page never writes

Bug 251 breaks a general rule: **no request, page load, or script run without a person may change data.**

- Wrapper and content GETs perform no writes. That includes view counters, which v1 does not have.
- **Write gate:** the runtime refuses writes until the page has received a trusted user interaction, meaning `pointerdown`, `keydown`, or `touchstart` with `isTrusted`, caught in the capture phase. Once that has happened, later writes are allowed, including timed autosaves. A refused write rejects with a clear runtime error (`TesselError: writes need a user interaction first`) and logs a console message aimed at authors and agents. Preview tools that run JavaScript load the page but never interact with it, so they can no longer vote. Neither can a page that votes on load.
- The sandboxed render runs the runtime in preview mode, which never persists anything.
- **Not in scope:** deliberate ballot-stuffing by someone scripting real interactions. That is abuse, handled by rate limits on runtime writes at the edge. The write gate protects against well-behaved automation, not attackers.
- **Assumed:** requiring an interaction before any write is acceptable for every page type. A page that has to write with no interaction at all, such as a pure visit counter, is not supported in v1.

I decided against sniffing user agents to hide the iframe from known preview tools. The list is never complete, it would have to vary the CDN cache by user agent, and the write gate fixes the problem for every client anyway.

## Verification before calling this done

- Paste a public page link and an unlisted page link into Slack, Discord, iMessage, and LinkedIn's post inspector while signed out. Each card shows the page title, the description, and the image. Use each tool's refresh after a change.
- Use a page whose body is built entirely by JavaScript. After publishing, its card shows the rendered title rather than a blank or the default.
- Fetch a private page with `curl` and no cookies, then with a member's cookie. Neither response contains the page's title, description, or `og:` tags. Open it in a browser as a member: the tab shows the real title and icon.
- Set a title and an icon (a `data:` SVG) in a page, publish, and check the tab. Change `document.title` at runtime and check that the tab follows, including after the wrapper re-renders.
- Paste a poll's link into five preview tools, including one that runs JavaScript, such as a headless-browser fetch. The vote count does not change. Voting by clicking still works.
- Make a public page private. The card URL returns `404` and the wrapper shows the generic head.

## Assumptions to confirm

1. Author HTML runs on a separate content origin in a sandboxed iframe. The cross-origin title relay and the security model depend on this.
2. The HTML file is the only authored source of metadata. v1 has no settings form for title, description, or image.
3. The tab and preview title format is `{page title} · Tessel`.
4. The default title comes from the page's address name, or is "Untitled page". I don't yet know the exact address format.
5. When static HTML lacks a title or description, a sandboxed headless render fills them in, for public and unlisted pages only.
6. The default preview image is a generated template card (Tessel mark, name, page title). There are no screenshots in v1, and an author's `og:image` wins.
7. A private page's title appears in the tab only for members, and only after client-side boot. The server HTML says "Tessel".
8. For non-members, a private page looks exactly like a nonexistent one. There is no "request access" flow in v1.
9. The runtime blocks writes until a trusted user interaction. Pages that write with no interaction at all are not supported.
10. The visibility control warns that previews other apps have already cached can't be recalled.
